well yes uh maros tanchos and joanna  gamolska have been jailed for a total of  25 years between them following a  lengthy three-month trial here at  bristol crown court it relates uh to  human trafficking which they carried out  for at least seven years here in the  city  tanchos and gamolska would travel to  orphanages  and  slum camps in slovakia to recruit  vulnerable mainly men on the promise of  a better life of work here in the uk  they would be brought over and promised  that they would be given accommodation  and half of their wages it was a very  different story when these victims  arrived in the city they were kept  inside a house in bristol uh made to  sleep on filthy mattresses uh to ten of  them uh in a room sharing uh one  bathroom they were locked inside uh the  house and none of the money of course  never went to them uh tanchos and  gomolska uh made them work day and night  in the days at a hand car wash on the  outskirts of the center of bristol and  various other jobs uh at night they were  always on the promise that they would um  get money eventually and they had come  in good faith to send money back to  their families but they were kept as  slaves and today at the judge told them  both in the dock that they preyed on  desperate and damaged people as they  were sentenced to 16 years tantros nine  years gamolska for trafficking  exploitation and forced labor offences  now this is an investigation which was  led by the national crime agency as well  as their counterparts in slovakia it  took a number of years  to  get those victims many of whom had  returned to slovakia to give evidence um  we have heard from one of the victims  who has given an interview anonymously  and this is what they had to say i was  living in an unknown country  i didn't know what to do  we had no life  for example if we wanted to go to the  shop we had to let him know  i knew i could not leave  there was nothing i could do i had to  stay we had no money we had no id  we had to stay  when i came back to slovakia i reported  him to the slovak police force  but they didn't believe me  also there wasn't enough evidence  against him and i want to make sure that  the same thing won't happen again to  other people  well uh tanchos and gamoska were  arrested in 2017  and that is when the investigation then  began in earliest and it emerged that  over the seven years or so that they  were running this human trafficking ring  the pair had netted nearly 300 000  pounds from the wages of course that  weren't passed on they took out credit  cards loans in the names of these  vulnerable people they would take them  to the banks  and set up bank accounts  but of course they kept control of all  of that money  throughout this operation uh mark  morrison from the national crime agency  has given that the organization's  reaction  we've got the whole range we've got  multiple victims 29 people prepared to  say that they were victims is a huge  number  we've got all the aggravating factors of  having their id cards withheld the  beatings the excessive hours um the  injuries one will say about his broken  arm where he had to go and get it fixed  and then he had him back working the  next day car washing with his unbroken  arm um  the  the mental anguish that these men and  women have gone through is absolutely  abhorrent  this was a couple who essentially saw  their victims as commodities in order to  make money for themselves they lived a  lavish lifestyle cars casinos uh online  gambling and that is what they spent  their money on the living conditions  that these  victims were made to live in uh were  absolutely squalid as i say filthy  mattresses on the floor the ncaa  interviewed more than 40 victims but  they believe there were many more  involved  in this exploitation ring which ran  between 2010 and  2017 and they are now starting prison  sentences totaling 25 years
 

